<Base
  title="About — Meghan Fabulous"
  description="Meghan Fabulous, born Meghan Noland: twenty-five years of wearable art, from a needle at four to a boat in the Pacific."
>
  <header class="page-head wrap">
    <p class="label">Story</p>
    <h1>The girl with a needle. <span class="butterfly" aria-hidden="true">🦋</span></h1>
  </header>
  <img
    class="lead"
    src="/images/picks/21_studio_meghan-at-rack.webp"
    alt="Meghan Fabulous pulling a piece from a rack in her studio."
    width="1244"
    height="1800"
  />
  <article class="wrap prose section">
    <p>
      My mom taught me to hand-sew when I was four. The machine came at six. Butterflies are for her. Every time I see
      one, I think of her.
    </p>
    <p>
      I went to FIT. I lasted until I knew everything. Which, obviously, was very quickly. A CFDA internship put me
      backstage for three seasons of New York shows: Ralph Lauren, Calvin Klein, Versace. I did not graduate. The joke
      is the point.
    </p>
    <h2>The name</h2>
    <p>
      Around 2003, in my mid-twenties, I lost the right to use Meghan Noland. A friend said, just change your name. It
      had to be something fabulous. That was the lightning. No one else needs to be in that sentence.
    </p>
    <h2>The runway</h2>
    <p>
      Los Angeles Fashion Week, 2004 to 2006. The B-Rad Dead dress. James Blonde. No-Tel Motel, which is where I said I
      put it in my Meghan blender, and then I pour my cocktail.
    </p>
    <h2>The world</h2>
    <p>
      Harrods, ASOS, Tokyo’s <em>Glitter</em>, more than thirty countries. Shakira in the Lulu Dress in
      <em>Harper’s Bazaar</em>. Mel B in the Shirley Dress at the 2008 VMAs.
    </p>
    <h2>The craft</h2>
    <p>
      I still sketch by hand. The LilyPad and the Jasmine each take more than seven yards. It’s my name on the door.
      The longer version is <a href="/journal/the-meghan-design-process">the design process</a>.
    </p>
    <h2>The Dead</h2>
    <p>
      A thrifted T-shirt became a dress. Paris Hilton wore it to the VH1 Big in ’04 Awards (December 2004, Shrine
      Auditorium, Los Angeles). Years later the clothes were officially licensed.
      <a href="/journal/the-paris-hilton-grateful-dead-dress">That story</a> is told in full, in words.
      <a href="https://liveforlivemusic.com/features/touch-of-glam-meghan-fabulous-grateful-dead/">See the photo</a>
      in James Sissler’s LiveForLiveMusic feature. Photograph by Jeff Kravitz.
    </p>
    <h2>The turn</h2>
    <p>
      I closed the business in 2025. On 3 December 2025, Steve and I departed Los Angeles aboard
      <em>Some Tuesday</em>, a Lagoon 450S, with Colonel Mustard and Mr. Pickles, the first pugs to cross the Pacific
      on a private sailing yacht.
    </p>
    <h2>Now</h2>
    <p>
      I make for joy, at sea. A health scare is in the journal, told the way I told it, without the photographs.
      <a href="/journal/skin-cancer-journey">My skin cancer journey</a>.
    </p>
    <p>Follow along if you want to. Nothing here is for sale.</p>
